# Target C — hostile audit of Sections 124--125

**Date:** 2026-09-12
**Base theorem state:** `2026-09-12.audit.46`
**Artifacts:** Sections 124 and 125 in `research/`
**Verdict:** **PASS at proof-only Type-II scope.**
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

## 1. Section 124: near-disk loaded-contact localization

Canonical Section 80 gives, after translation,

\[
d_H(F_D,B(0,D/2))=o(D).
\]

For `delta_D` equal to this Hausdorff error, convex support-function control gives

\[
B(0,D/2-\delta_D)
\subset F_D
\subset B(0,D/2+\delta_D).
\]

At every boundary point, every supporting normal differs from the local radial
direction by at most

\[
\beta_D=
\arccos{D/2-\delta_D\over D/2+\delta_D}=o(1).
\]

If two loaded vertices are at distance one, their radial directions differ by
`O(1/D)`. Radial projection is cyclic-order preserving because the origin is interior,
so the short polar boundary arc between them has all side directions in an interval of
width `o(1)`. If the vertices were nonconsecutive, that arc would contain at least two
sides, each of length at least one. Projection onto the midpoint direction would give

\[
1\ge2\cos(o(1))>1,
\]

contradiction. Thus every loaded-loaded unit contact is a loaded-hull side.

The argument is uniform, does not require a rate for the Hausdorff convergence, and
does not use smoothness. Therefore

\[
c_B=0,
\qquad e_{BB}=q-m
\]

is valid for all sufficiently large Type-II survivors.

**Section-124 verdict:** PASS.

## 2. Section 125: classification of augmented triangles

Canonical Section 123's `J_r^+` starts from internal/attachment contacts and adds the
attachment-polygon boundary. A bounded triangular face must use at least one polygon
side because the old graph is contact-triangle-free.

A face with two polygon sides would have a third loaded-loaded contact joining the
endpoints of a polygon ear. If `|A_r|>3`, those endpoints are nonconsecutive globally,
contradicting Section 124. If `|A_r|=3`, the triangle is the outer polygon and contains
the nonempty component `I_r`, so it is not a bounded face. Three polygon sides are
excluded identically. Hence exactly one side is added.

If the third vertex were loaded, its two contact edges would be global hull sides by
Section 124; inherited cyclic order again forces the three-attachment outer-boundary
case. Hence the third vertex is unloaded. The base length lies in `(1,2]`.

This verifies Lemma 125.1.

## 3. An augmented-triangle base cannot be an internal loaded chord

For every loaded chord of length at most two, the same near-disk argument as Section
124 gives a short polar arc whose side-direction width `tau_D` tends uniformly to
zero.

If the base endpoints were nonconsecutive globally, projection gives

\[
|a-b|\ge j\cos(\tau_D/2)
\]

for the number `j>=2` of hull sides on that short arc. Since `|a-b|<=2`, eventually
`j=2`. Let `y` be the intermediate loaded vertex, with side lengths `ell_1,ell_2`.
Then

\[
|a-b|\to2,
\qquad
\ell_1,\ell_2\to1.
\]

For the midpoint `M=(a+b)/2`, the unloaded apex `x` obeys

\[
|x-M|^2=1-|a-b|^2/4\to0,
\]

while the exact median identity gives

\[
|y-M|^2
={\ell_1^2+\ell_2^2\over2}-{|a-b|^2\over4}
\to0.
\]

Hence `|x-y|->0`, contradicting source unit separation. The argument does not assume
which side of the base contains `x` or `y`.

Thus every augmented triangular face is based on a nonunit global hull side.

## 4. Global injectivity of the side charge

Within one cell, a polygon side borders at most one bounded face. Across two distinct
cells, the same global hull side cannot be a side of both: both two-dimensional convex
cells lie in the same inward half-plane of the supporting line and therefore overlap
in positive area near the relative interior of the shared side. This contradicts
canonical Section-83 interior disjointness.

Therefore

\[
T:=\sum_rT_r\le m.
\]

## 5. Algebraic verification of the sharpened payment

Canonical Section 123 gives

\[
e_{BB}-k
={1\over2}(a-T+Q^+)+d_{tile},
\qquad
d_{tile}=q-2-a+2c_I.
\]

Section 124 and canonical Section 63 give

\[
e_{BB}=q-m=Q+2k+u_B.
\]

Eliminating `a` gives exactly

\[
Q^+
=Q+u_B-(m-T)-2(c_I-1)-d_{tile}.
\]

In Type II, `c_I>=1` for all sufficiently large `D`: the positive core has
`Theta(D^2)` vertices while the loaded set has only `O(D)`. Hence

\[
Q^+\le Q+u_B.
\]

Canonical Section 123 has `P_exc,r<=5Q_r^+`, so

\[
\sum_rP_{{\rm exc},r}
\le5(Q+u_B)
=5(e_{BB}-2k).
\]

This is finite exact algebra; no asymptotic interchange occurs.

## 6. Composition with canonical Section 122

Canonical Section 122 gives

\[
\liminf_D{1\over D}
\sum_r(L_r+P_{{\rm exc},r}-2d_r)
\ge {\pi\over2}(2-\sqrt3).
\]

Replacing `P_exc` by the pointwise upper bound above increases the left-hand quantity,
so

\[
\liminf_D{1\over D}
\left[
\sum_r(L_r-2d_r)+5(Q+u_B)
\right]
\ge {\pi\over2}(2-\sqrt3)
\]

is valid.

